{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Moses: Come to Pharaoh, etc.</b> Many have wondered about this passage, that it does not mention what Moses is to say to Pharaoh when he comes to him, for nothing of the plague of locusts is mentioned. And what the Ramban explained in the name of the Rabbenu Tam — that there is an allusion to the plague of locusts in what is said, ״וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וּבֶן בִנְךָ אֵת אֲשֶׁר הִתְעַלַּלְתִּי בְּמִצְרַיִם״ (Exodus 10:2), and it is written of locusts in Joel ״עָלֶיהָ לִבְנֵיכֶם סַפֵּרוּ וּבְנֵיכֶם לִבְנֵיהֶם״ — this is not sufficient to resolve the difficulty, for the very thing itself is the difficulty: why is it said of this plague in particular that one should tell it to one's children more than all the other plagues? And I have a further difficulty: why is the hardening of his servants' hearts mentioned only in the plague of hail and the plague of locusts?\\n\\nAnd according to its plain sense, it appears to me that in all the plagues there was certainly no distinction between Pharaoh and his servants, for certainly Pharaoh felt every one of them just as his servants did — except for the plague of famine and drought, which touches only the servants but not the king himself, for as a rule kings have storehouses of grain against years of famine, and it is only by way of extraordinary measure that the famine would reach the king himself. Therefore it says of the hail, ״וַיַּכְבֵּד לִבּוֹ הוּא וַעֲבָדָיו״ (Exodus 9:34) — not only he, but even his servants, who should have taken heed that HaShem had called for famine, nevertheless did not pay heed, because the wheat and the spelt were not struck. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, said a second time to Moses, ״כִּי אֲנִי הִכְבַּדְתִּי אֶת לִבּוֹ וְאֶת לֵב עֲבָדָיו״ (Exodus 10:1), Moses understood from this that He would make for him a second warning, for HaShem had called for famine, and even so they would not set their hearts to His warning — not only he, but even his servants. And from this Moses understood that the will of HaShem, blessed be He, was to send the locusts, for it is the way of the world that produce that has finished growing in the field has no destruction except by means of hail or by means of locusts, and since there had already been hail, it could only be locusts that would eat what the hail had left. Therefore it is said of this plague specifically, ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו עַבְדֵי פַרְעֹה עַד מָתַי יִהְיֶה זֶה לָנוּ לְמוֹקֵשׁ״ (Exodus 10:7), for the famine touched his servants more than Pharaoh; therefore they urged Pharaoh to send the people." "And as for what is said of this plague — ״וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וּבֶן בִנְךָ״ — more than all the other plagues, behold I have room here to resolve this according to the way the Ramban wrote in this passage in the name of Rabbenu Hananel on the verse ״וְאַחֲרָיו לֹא יִהְיֶה כֵּן״ (Exodus 10:14), and this is his language: 'From the prayer of Moses until now, locusts have not caused damage in all the territory of Egypt, and if they fall in the land of Israel and enter the territory of Egypt, they do not eat any of Egypt's produce at all — and this is known to all, and of this it is said ״שִׂיחוּ בְּכָל נִפְלְאוֹתָיו״ (Psalms 105:2).' Thus far his language. And look to the right and see, for the verse ״שִׂיחוּ בְּכָל נִפְלְאוֹתָיו״ is exactly like the verse ״וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וּבֶן בִנְךָ״.\\n\\nAnd the explanation of the matter is that in all the plagues that were in Egypt, no trace of them remained in Egypt after their removal that would cause future generations to ask the children — 'what is this' and 'what is this' — for their memory was lost among the masses, because the days grew long for them and everything was forgotten. But in the plague of locusts a trace remained for generations even after the removal of the plague, for in the course of many days the generations will find a new thing and will see that locusts come and do not eat anything at all of all the produce of Egypt. Then the children will surely ask about this wonder — why does it not eat of the produce of Egypt but only of the produce of the land of Israel? And perforce you will need to answer him with the account of everything that happened in the land of Egypt, and that Moses said at the removal of the plague that no locusts should remain in all the territory of Egypt, and through this they will recount the miracles of Egypt and recognize the power of His kingdom, blessed be He. And this is what Rabbenu Hananel concludes in his words with the verse ״שִׂיחוּ בְּכָל נִפְלְאוֹתָיו״, for without doubt it was for this reason that the Holy One, blessed be He, left this trace for generations, so that through it they would recall the exodus from Egypt and speak of all His wonders. This is what it says: ״וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וגו׳, וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״ — for through this account the coming generations will know His might, blessed be He, and His wonders. And this explanation is very precious and correct.\\n\\nAnd as for what is said in Joel of locusts, ״סַפְּרוּ לִבְנֵיכֶם״ etc. (Joel 1:3), this is no difficulty, for it is certainly fitting to recount to generations how much sin brings upon Israel, so that future generations may take instruction. But here the explanation is surely not so, for if so, why did He command that one tell his children specifically of the plague of locusts more than all the plagues? Rather, surely the reason is as we have mentioned." "And as for why the plague of locusts was not mentioned explicitly but only by allusion — it is because the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted Moses to rebuke Pharaoh for his folly, that he would never submit to the word of HaShem but only because of the plagues that had already come. And the repentance that a person does under compulsion is not complete repentance, for when the compelling force is removed he will return to his corruption, as happened to Pharaoh. Therefore He did not mention the plague explicitly, so that Moses would understand from this that he should begin with reproof for his sin, and demand of him that he submit to the word of HaShem and not to the plague, and then he would remain in his righteousness. And so Moses did, for the beginning of his words was ״עַד מָתַי מֵאַנְתָּ לֵעָנֹת מִפָּנָי״ (Exodus 10:3) — meaning, from before the word of HaShem and not from before the plague. Therefore ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו עַבְדֵי פַרְעֹה עַד מָתַי יִהְיֶה זֶה לָנוּ לְמוֹקֵשׁ״ (Exodus 10:7), and they wanted to make a compromise with Moses before the plague's arrival according to Moses' word, and his servants entered first into the thickness of the beam for the reason explained above."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The text reads הָרַבָּתִי ('the Rabboti'); I have resolved it as a reference to Rabbenu Tam based on the immediately following attribution and context, though the identification is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:10.125899733Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}